


@@BEGIN: PRESENTATION
@id: poudrier
@title: Modeling Polyrhythmic Experience: A Corpus of 20th-Century Polyphonic Music
@presenter: poudrier
@abstract: 

This presentation features an interdisciplinary research
project that investigated listeners’ experience of
complex rhythmic structures characteristic of the first
half of the 20th-century music in Europe and North America.
Harnessing advances in computational representation and
analysis of music, the <i>Polyrhythm Project</i>
(https://polyrhythm.humdrum.org) offers a digital corpus of
725 polyrhythmic excerpts drawn from 450 works by 20
representative composers. Following best practices in
digital scholarship, each example includes over seventy
metadata fields—spanning duration, time signature, tempo,
instrumentation, and music production. The corpus is
published as an open-access web platform with detailed
documentation, including references to scores and
recordings used during the encoding and analysis phases of
the project. Users can interactively filter and browse
examples by metadata, explore music production with
interactive maps, visualize composite and coincidence
rhythms that highlight polyrhythmic features, and listen to
high-quality synthetic audio generated using EastWest
Sounds libraries. Hosted on GitHub Pages with a
Leaflet-based mapping interface and dynamic data
visualizations, the platform supports public access and
future extension for research, pedagogical, and creative
applications.

To quantify rhythmic complexity, we developed a composite
analysis tool that extracts event onsets and computes
measures of polyrhythmic activity overall and between
parts, all integrated into the online platform. These data
supported the design of a series of experimental studies
with ecological stimuli that aimed to characterize
listeners’ perception of four emotional dimensions (mood,
energy, movement, dissonance, and tension). The findings
point to significant effects of selected rhythmic variables
unveiled by the composite tool, such as the nested ratio
(proportion of coinciding events across parts), event
density ratio (calculated in notes per second in human
performance), and the differential in note-to-note
variability between component rhythmic layers. Effects of
pitch-related features such as sonority dissonance and
pitch height were also observed.

The presentation will conclude with an introduction of
“Folklore vivant,” a new research and public outreach
project for community resilience through creative
engagement with Francophone songs. Both projects
demonstrate how new technologies can enhance research
practices, knowledge mobilization, and accessible
dissemination, and thus contribute to preservation of, and
creative interaction with, diverse musical cultures.



@@END: PRESENTATION



